mention": "Histone proteins", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.979726254940033, "span": {"begin": 939, "end": 955}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:54145"], "is_neural_normalized": true, "mention": "histones", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9830948710441589, "span": {"begin": 1037, "end": 1045}}, {"id": ["CHEBI:32875"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "methyl", "obj": "drug", "prob": 0.9993813037872314, "span": {"begin": 1081, "end": 1087}}, {"id": ["CHEBI:46887"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "acetyl", "obj": "drug", "prob": 0.9982888102531433, "span": {"begin": 1089, "end": 1095}}, {"id": ["mesh:D010710"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "phosphate", "obj": "drug", "prob": 0.9982728958129883, "span": {"begin": 1100, "end": 1109}}], "text": "Estrogen receptors (ER) are nuclear transcription factors involved in regulation of many physiological processes in vertebrates through signalling. &nbsp;There are two main types of estrogen receptors, estrogen receptor alpha (ER&alpha;) and estrogen receptor beta (ER&beta;) receptors. &nbsp;Binding by estrogen activates estrogen receptor activity. &nbsp;Suppression of estrogen receptor activity occurs when a stressor hinders estrogen binding to estrogen receptors or when estrogen levels are diminished, resulting in lowered estrogen receptor activity.   Epigenetic modifications are alterations that affect gene expression without altering the underlying gene sequence. &nbsp;Among some of the more common processes that lead to epigenetic modifications are DNA methylation and alteration of histone proteins. &nbsp;DNA methylation involves addition of methyl groups to DNA which causes increased or decreased gene expression. &nbsp;Histone proteins are small proteins that assist gene expression, DNA repair, and DNA replication; histones can be modified by the addition of methyl, acetyl, or phosphate groups, which accelerates or slows DNA expression, repair, and replication.&nbsp; &nbsp;", "timestamp": "Tue Oct 13 16:00:15 +0000 2026"}